<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  AC-g1: a manual horizontal scroll suspends playhead-following for 5 seconds
  (SMVActualView::FOLLOW_HOLD_MS). This is a WALL-CLOCK case (RUN_SERIAL,
  real-time playback via the capture backend — no SMARAGD_CAPTURE_SPEED
  override, since the hold itself is measured against a real QElapsedTimer).

  `wheel-scroll` is a new minimal testkit verb (main/testkit/src/
  sclicklaneaction.cpp): it sends a REAL QWheelEvent to the arranger canvas,
  through SMainWindow::wheelScrollArranger -> SStdMixerView::wheelScroll,
  landing in the actual wheelEvent()/applyWheel() path — including
  armFollowHold() — rather than a re-spelling of "pan the view"
  (set-lane-view's scrollX= bypasses every arm point ON PURPOSE, so a case
  using it could not tell the hold apart from doing nothing at all).

  THE PAN DISTANCE IS NOT ASSERTED, AND THAT IS THE POINT. This case used to
  assert `scrollX="2580"` after the wheel notch, described as its "closed
  form", measured by running the setup repeatedly before committing it. That
  number was never a property of the code under test:

      step = (visibleSpan / 8) * wheelSensitivity_        (applyWheel)

  and `wheelSensitivity_` is a USER SETTING — SOpt::WheelSensitivityPct, Edit
  -> Options -> Mouse, persisted in smaragd.ini as
  `[mouse] wheelSensitivityPercent`. The case therefore passed only on a box
  left at the 100 % default, and it had been FAILING on the author's own
  machine, where that setting is 160 %: canvas 430 px at secondWidth=1000
  gives span 20640 and span/8 = 2580, and the run measured 2580 * 1.6 = 4128
  exactly. Not a flake, not a regression, and nothing to do with the hold.

  So the three checks below are RELATIVE (`snapshot` / `compareTo`, the same
  pair assert-envelope already uses). They say what AC-g1 actually claims —
  the view moved, then did not move, then moved again — without naming a
  distance the user is free to change. `minScrollX="1"` keeps the first check
  honest: the wheel must have done SOMETHING, or "unchanged" two seconds later
  would be vacuous.

  (The canvas geometry note that used to live here still holds and still
  matters for the grab: the canvas is never SHOWN in a headless run, and
  `assert-lane-alignment grabWidth="800"` resizes the WINDOW to fit an 800px
  canvas while the canvas itself lands narrower — 430 px as measured — once
  the layout subtracts the track-control column, scrollbar and margins. It is
  simply no longer load-bearing for any assertion.)

  WHY THE HOLD IS ACTUALLY BEING TESTED, not just "nothing moved because
  nothing would have anyway": at scrollX=2580 and secondWidth=1000, the
  playhead crosses followLocator's 80% forward-edge trigger at roughly frame
  19000 — under half a second of real playback — so during either wait-ms
  window below the transport crosses it many times over. Without the hold,
  ordinary follow would have re-paged long before either check runs.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="follow_scroll_hold">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- One empty track: proposal 09 - the MASTER root's playhead is always
         "sounding" regardless of content (localPlayhead() takes the early
         return for an empty root name), so followLocator needs no audible
         material to be exercised. -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>

    <set-lane-view secondWidth="1000"/>
    <!-- Forces the never-shown canvas to a known, reproducible size (see the
         header) — a free lane-alignment check comes along with it. -->
    <assert-lane-alignment grabPng="follow_hold_canvas.png"
                           grabWidth="800" grabHeight="200"/>

    <set-locator position="0"/>
    <toggle-playback play="1"/>
    <wait-playhead minAdvance="4096" timeoutMs="30000"/>

    <!-- A REAL wheel notch, Shift+wheel = Pan under the default mapping
         (SOpt::WheelShift). This ARMS the 5 s hold. -->
    <wheel-scroll deltaY="-600" modifiers="shift"/>
    <!-- The view MOVED, and we remember where to - we do not name the number.
         See the header: one notch pans span/8 * wheelSensitivity_, and that
         sensitivity is a USER SETTING. -->
    <assert-lane-view minScrollX="1" snapshot="held"/>

    <!-- Well inside the hold window: the transport has had ~2 s to cross the
         forward edge repeatedly, and the view must not have moved. -->
    <wait-ms ms="2000"/>
    <assert-lane-view compareTo="held"/>

    <!-- ~7 s since the scroll, 2 s past the 5 s hold with margin: follow has
         resumed and re-paged AT LEAST once, so the view is no longer sitting
         at the held position. -->
    <wait-ms ms="5000"/>
    <assert-lane-view compareTo="held" expectReject="true"/>

    <toggle-playback play="0"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
